#> tool: sedrepl
#> wait: 4 dates 16
#> pause: 40
#> must: 3 cherries 7
#> must: 4 dates 16
#> not: 5 figs 25
#> source: /dates/q
# 14. q and Q. Sheet: "q" quits; extension "Q" quits at once without printing.
# q prints the pattern space (dates), then stops reading: figs to lemons are never
# seen. That is what makes "sed 10q" a cheap head. Q is the GNU variant that quits
# without printing the current line, so it would stop after cherries.
# Try: change q to Q and the dates line is gone too. Then try 2q, and 2Q.
/dates/q

#DATA
1 apples 12
2 bananas 30
3 cherries 7
4 dates 16
5 figs 25
6 grapes 40
7 kiwis 18
8 lemons 22
